.TH SSOOSSHD.YAML 5 "2026-08-16" "ssoossh" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
ssoosshd.yaml \- configuration file for the ssoosshd server
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ssoosshd.yaml
is a YAML file configuring
.BR ssoosshd (8):
the HTTP(S) listener, TLS, the database, OIDC authentication, optional LDAP
enrichment, and the certificate-issuance policy for each certificate type
ssoosshd signs.
.SH LOCATION
Built-in defaults are loaded first (compiled into the binary; not a file on
disk), then exactly one config file is merged on top. If
.BR "ssoosshd --config " \fIfile\fR
is given, that file is used. Otherwise the following are searched, in
order, and the
.B first one found
is used \(em unlike the client's
.BR ssoossh.yaml (5),
these are not all merged together, so only one file's settings actually
apply:
.IP 1. 4
.I ./ssoosshd.yaml
.IP 2. 4
.I ~/.config/ssoosshd.yaml
.IP 3. 4
.I ~/.config/ssoossh/ssoosshd.yaml
.IP 4. 4
.I /etc/ssoosshd.yaml
.IP 5. 4
.I /etc/ssoossh/ssoosshd.yaml
.SH OPTIONS
Keys are shown as their full dotted path, e.g.
.B http.tls.min_version
means:
.RS
.nf
http:
  tls:
    min_version: ...
.fi
.RE
Every key below is top-level (or nested only as shown) in the actual config
schema; some older example files in this repository nest
.B ldap
and
.B authentication
under
.BR http: ,
which does not match the schema and is silently ignored \(em see
.B NOTES
below.
.SS Top level
.TP
.BI production: " bool"
General production-mode flag. Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI traces: " bool"
Enable tracing. Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI metrics: " bool"
Enable metrics. Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI ssh_key: " string"
The CA's private key, inline, in OpenSSH private-key PEM format (e.g. the
output of
.BR "ssh-keygen -t ed25519" ).
.BR Required ;
the server fails to start if it is missing or unparseable. This is the
only certificate-signing key source currently implemented \(em signing
always happens with this key loaded directly into the server process; there
is no ssh-agent-based or HSM/KMS-based signing backend yet, even though the
signing pipeline is built behind an interface that could support one later.
Never log or otherwise expose this value.
.SS logging (main application log)
.TP
.BI logging.level: " string"
Minimum log level: a level name (
.IR debug ", " info ", " warn ", " error ,
case-insensitive, optionally with a numeric
.B +N
/
.B -N
offset) or a raw numeric slog level (e.g.
.IR -4 ", " 0 ", " 4 ", " 8 ).
Unrecognized or empty values fall back to
.BR info .
Default:
.BR WARN .
.TP
.BI logging.enable_stdout: " bool"
Also write the main log to stdout even when
.B logging.filename
is set. (When
.B logging.filename
is unset, or the process is attached to a terminal, stdout is used
regardless of this setting.) Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI logging.include_app_name: " bool"
Add an
.B app
attribute (the string
.RB \(dq ssoossh \(dq )
to every log record. Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI logging.include_app_version: " bool"
Add a
.B version
attribute to every log record. Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI logging.log_json: " bool"
Write JSON instead of colorized/plain text. Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI logging.filename , " logging.maxsize" , " logging.maxage" , " logging.maxbackups" , " logging.localtime" , " logging.compression" , " logging.rotationinterval" , " logging.backuptimeformat" , " logging.rotateatminutes" , " logging.rotateat" , " logging.appendtimeafterext" , " logging.filemode"
Log-file rotation, via the embedded
.B timberjack
logger. Unset
.B filename
means no log file is written (output goes to stdout instead, per
.BR enable_stdout " above); "
.B maxsize
is in megabytes (default 100);
.B maxage
is in days (default: keep forever);
.B maxbackups
caps retained rotated files (default: keep all, subject to
.BR maxage );
.B localtime
uses local time instead of UTC in rotated filenames;
.B compression
is
.IR none ", " gzip ", or " zstd ;
.B rotationinterval
is a duration (e.g.
.IR 24h )
forcing rotation on a schedule in addition to size; the remaining fields
tune backup filename formatting and rotation-at-specific-times. See each
field's doc comment in the
.B DeRuina/timberjack
package for full detail.
.SS db (database)
.TP
.BI db.provider: " sqlite" | postgres
Database backend. Default:
.BR sqlite .
.TP
.BI db.connection_string: " string"
For
.BR sqlite ,
a file path (or
.B :memory:
for in-memory). For
.BR postgres ,
a standard
.B postgres://
URL or keyword string. Default:
.BR ssoossh.db .
.TP
.BI db.retry_attempts: " int"
Maximum connection attempts before giving up. Zero or negative disables
retries. Default:
.BR 3 .
.TP
.BI db.retry_interval: " duration"
Wait between retry attempts; only used when
.B retry_attempts
> 1. Default:
.BR 3s .
.TP
.B db.logging.*
Independent log destination for database queries, same fields as
.B logging.*
above except it has no
.B enable_stdout
/
.B include_app_name
/
.B include_app_version
(instead:
.B db.logging.add_source
adds source file/line to each record; default
.BR false ).
Routed by a
.RB \(dq type=db \(dq
attribute; only takes effect once
.B db.logging.filename
is set.
.SS http (listener, TLS, sessions, rate limiting)
.TP
.BI http.address: " string"
Interface to bind, e.g.
.I 127.0.0.1
for local-only or
.I 0.0.0.0
for all interfaces. Ignored when
.B http.unix_socket
is set. Default:
.BR 127.0.0.1 .
.TP
.BI http.port: " int"
TCP port to listen on. 0 tells the OS to pick an unused port (the actual
port is logged at startup); this is intended for testing. Ignored when
.B http.unix_socket
is set. Default:
.BR 8080 .
.TP
.BI http.unix_socket: " path"
Listen on a Unix domain socket at this path instead of TCP;
.B address
/
.B port
are then ignored. Mutually exclusive with
.B http.proxy_protocol
(PROXY protocol is a TCP concept). Default: unset.
.TP
.BI http.proxy_protocol: " list"
CIDR ranges of reverse proxies trusted to prefix connections with a PROXY
protocol v1/v2 header, so the real client address survives a TCP-level
proxy. Empty (default) disables PROXY protocol entirely; once set,
connections from any other source are rejected outright. Ignored when
.B http.unix_socket
is set.
.TP
.BI http.trusted_proxies: " list"
CIDR ranges of reverse proxies trusted to set
.B X-Forwarded-For
/
.BR X-Forwarded-Proto .
Empty (default) means no proxy is trusted: the client IP always reflects
the direct connection, and a client-supplied
.B X-Forwarded-For
is ignored.
.TP
.BI http.server_name: " string"
Host name this server answers to. A request addressed to anything else (by
.B Host
header, or SNI on a TLS connection) is rejected with
.B 421
.RB ( "Misdirected Request" ),
except the health-check endpoints, which are exempt so probes can reach the
server by IP. Empty (default) disables the check. Required by the OIDC
service unless
.B http.public_url
is set instead.
.TP
.BI http.public_url: " url"
The scheme and host browsers actually reach this deployment at, e.g.
.IR https://ssh.example.com .
Set this whenever it differs from what
.BR address / port /TLS
describe, which is every reverse-proxy deployment. Two things depend on it:
the OIDC redirect URI handed to the identity provider, and the origin
compared against the browser's
.B Origin
header for CSRF protection. Must be an origin only \(em no path, query, or
fragment; a trailing slash is tolerated and trimmed. Validated at startup:
an invalid value fails the server before it starts, rather than surfacing
later as an identity-provider-side redirect rejection. Default: unset
(falls back to being derived from
.BR server_name ", " port ", and TLS status" ).
.TP
.BI http.is_https: " bool"
Set when a reverse proxy in front of this process terminates TLS, so this
process itself runs plain HTTP but must still be treated as HTTPS for
session-cookie and redirect-URI purposes. Not needed if
.B http.public_url
is already set with an
.B https://
scheme. Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.cookie_key: " string"
Secret used to sign and encrypt session cookies. If empty (default), a key
is generated once and persisted in the database, so sessions survive a
restart and every instance sharing a database shares the key. Set this
explicitly to key it from outside the database instead.
.TP
.BI http.cookie_secure: " bool"
Marks the session cookie
.BR Secure .
Unset (default) derives it from whether the deployment is HTTPS at all (see
.BR http.public_url / is_https );
set explicitly only to override that inference.
.TP
.BI http.cookie_same_site: " strict" | lax | none
Session cookie
.B SameSite
attribute. Default:
.BR strict ,
appropriate here since nothing legitimately navigates into this server from
another site.
.TP
.BI http.cookie_max_age: " duration"
Session cookie lifetime. Zero uses the session store's own default.
.TP
.BI http.rate_limit: " int"
Maximum requests per
.B http.rate_duration
per client IP. Zero or negative (default) disables rate limiting.
Localhost (127.0.0.1, ::1) always bypasses the limit.
.TP
.BI http.rate_duration: " duration"
Time window for
.BR http.rate_limit .
.TP
.BI http.hsts: " string"
Value of the
.B Strict-Transport-Security
header, sent only when this process terminates TLS itself (a certificate is
configured under
.BR http.tls ).
Empty disables the header \(em appropriate when a TLS-terminating reverse
proxy sets its own policy instead. Example:
.RB \(dq "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains" \(dq .
Default: unset.
.TP
.B http.tls.certificate " / " http.tls.private_key
Inline PEM certificate (chain) and private key. Both must be set together;
takes precedence over the
.B _file
variants below.
.TP
.B http.tls.certificate_file " / " http.tls.private_key_file
Paths to PEM certificate and private key files, read once at startup.
Consulted only when the inline pair above is incomplete. If neither pair is
complete, the server runs without TLS: plain HTTP with HTTP/2 cleartext
(h2c) enabled.
.TP
.BI http.tls.min_version: " string"
Minimum TLS protocol version:
.IR TLS1.0 ", " TLS1.1 ", " TLS1.2 ", or " TLS1.3 .
Parsing tolerates case and
.B .
/
.B -
/
.B _
/space separators, and
.BR tls / version
prefixes (e.g.
.IR tls12 ", " TLS-1.2 ", " VersionTLS12
all work).
.B TLS1.0
and
.B TLS1.1
are accepted but log a deprecation warning (RFC 8996). Default (in the
shipped
.IR _defaults.yaml ):
.BR TLS1.3 .
.TP
.BI http.tls.cipher_suites: " list"
TLS 1.0\-1.2 cipher suite names (Go does not allow configuring TLS 1.3
suites), matched exactly and case-sensitively against
.B crypto/tls
constant names, e.g.
.BR TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 .
Empty (default) uses Go's defaults. Any explicit list must include
.B TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
or
.BR TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 ,
or the server fails to start \(em net/http's automatic HTTP/2 setup
requires one of them.
.TP
.BI http.tls.curves: " list"
Key-exchange curve/group preference order:
.IR P256 ", " P384 ", " P521 ", " X25519 ", or " X25519MLKEM768 ,
with the same tolerant name parsing as
.BR min_version .
Empty (default) uses Go's defaults; setting any curve replaces the defaults
entirely rather than adding to them.
.TP
.B http.access_logging.*
Which fields the HTTP access log records: boolean switches
.BR log_user_agent ", " log_request_header ", " log_client_ip ", "
.BR log_request_id ", " log_request_body ", " log_response_body ", "
.BR log_response_header ", " log_span_id ", and " log_trace_id ,
plus
.B log_json
and the shared
.B timberjack
rotation fields (see
.B logging.*
above). Access logging has no separate level knob (it is always effectively
.BR info )
and never includes source file/line. Routed by a
.RB \(dq type=accesslog \(dq
attribute; only takes effect once
.B http.access_logging.filename
is set, otherwise it merges into the general log stream. Defaults (shipped
.IR _defaults.yaml ):
.BR log_user_agent ", " log_request_header ", and " log_client_ip
true; the rest false.
.SS authentication (OIDC)
.TP
.BI authentication.client_id: " string"
.B Required.
OAuth2/OIDC client ID registered with the identity provider.
.TP
.BI authentication.client_secret: " string"
OAuth2 client secret.
.TP
.BI authentication.provider_url: " url"
.B Required.
The OIDC provider's issuer URL, used for discovery
.RI ( /.well-known/openid-configuration
is stripped automatically if present in the value).
.TP
.BI authentication.scopes: " string"
Space-separated additional scopes requested alongside the always-included
.B openid
scope, e.g.
.RB \(dq "profile email" \(dq .
Default (Go zero value, i.e. no additional scopes) unless explicitly set
\(em see
.B NOTES
below.
.TP
.B authentication.fields.*
Maps ssoossh identity fields to OIDC ID-token claim names.
.RS
.TP
.BI username: " string"
.B Required.
Claim holding the local account username, e.g.
.RB \(dq preferred_username \(dq .
.TP
.BI groups: " string"
Claim expected to hold a JSON array of group names. Feeds the certificate
lifetime/require-group decision only; group membership is never placed in
an issued certificate.
.TP
.BI other_accounts: " string"
Claim expected to hold a JSON array of alternate account identifiers this
identity is known by on target systems (a different username, UPN, or
.BR sAMAccountName ),
added to a certificate's principal list alongside
.BR username .
.TP
.BI service_accounts: " string"
Claim expected to hold a JSON array of service-account identifiers this
identity is authorized to manage/enroll certificates for.
.TP
.BI email: " string"
Claim holding the user's email. Empty falls back to the standard
.B email
claim opportunistically (not an error if that is absent too).
.RE
.SS ldap (optional identity enrichment)
.B "Schema is parsed but not yet consumed by the server"
\(em setting these has no effect on issued certificates or authentication
today. Documented for forward compatibility with the config format only.
.TP
.BI ldap.enabled: " bool"
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI ldap.url , " ldap.bind_dn" , " ldap.bind_password" , " ldap.base_dn" , " ldap.user_filter" : " string"
Connection and search parameters for a future LDAP lookup keyed by the
username resolved from OIDC.
.TP
.B ldap.logging.*
Independent log destination for LDAP activity, same shape as
.B db.logging.*
above. Routed by a
.RB \(dq type=ldap \(dq
attribute; only takes effect once
.B ldap.logging.filename
is set. (This routing is wired up regardless of whether LDAP enrichment
itself is consumed.)
.SS queue
.TP
.B queue.logging.*
Independent log destination for the internal pub/sub message queue, same
shape as
.B db.logging.*
above. Routed by a
.RB \(dq type=queue \(dq
attribute; only takes effect once
.B queue.logging.filename
is set.
.SS cert_options (issuance policy)
.TP
.BI cert_options.request_ttl: " duration"
How long a pending certificate request (user, host, service, or PAM) stays
open for approval before it is treated as expired. Shared across every
type. Default:
.BR 5m .
.TP
.BI cert_options.signing_timeout: " duration"
How long an approved request may sit awaiting signature before the sweep
treats it as stranded and fails it. Measured from the request's creation
time, offset by
.BR request_ttl ,
not from approval. Default:
.BR 5m .
.TP
.B cert_options.user.*
Policy for interactive user certificates.
.RS
.TP
.BI require_group: " string"
OIDC group an approver must belong to. Empty (default) means any
authenticated user may approve any request \(em this only governs who may
.I approve
certificates in general; approval is separately bound to being the
specific request's own requester or in the approver chain.
.TP
.BI valid_duration: " duration"
Certificate lifetime. Default (shipped
.IR _defaults.yaml ,
a development value):
.BR 30s .
.TP
.BI extensions: " list"
SSH certificate extensions granted, e.g.
.BR permit-pty ", " permit-agent-forwarding ", " permit-port-forwarding ", "
.BR permit-X11-forwarding ", " permit-user-rc .
Default:
.BR "[permit-pty, permit-user-rc]" .
.TP
.BI key_id_template: " string"
Go
.B text/template
string executed against issuance context to produce the certificate's key
ID. Empty uses a built-in default. This is also the fallback used by
.BR cert_options.service " and " cert_options.host
when their own
.B key_id_template
is empty (but
.B not
by
.BR cert_options.pam ,
which never falls back to it \(em see below).
.RE
.TP
.B cert_options.service.*
Policy for non-interactive service certificates: same
.BR require_group ", " valid_duration ", " extensions ", " key_id_template
shape as
.BR cert_options.user .
Defaults:
.B require_group
empty,
.B valid_duration
.BR 8760h " (one year), " extensions
empty.
.TP
.B cert_options.host.*
Policy for host certificates:
.BR require_group ", " valid_duration ", " key_id_template
(no
.B extensions
\(em host certificates do not carry interactive-session extensions).
Defaults:
.B require_group
empty,
.B valid_duration
.BR 8760h .
.TP
.B cert_options.pam.*
Policy for PAM certificates (short-lived certificates a
.BR pam_ssoossh (8)-authenticated
local operation, e.g.
.BR sudo (8),
validates once and discards). Same
.B require_group / valid_duration / extensions / key_id_template
shape as
.BR cert_options.user ,
but with deliberately different defaults and fallback behavior:
.RS
.TP
.BI require_group: " string"
Unlike every other
.BR require_group ,
.B empty here means no PAM certificates are ever issued
rather than "any authenticated user may approve." An operator must set
this explicitly to enable PAM issuance at all \(em "who may sudo on this
host" is a narrower question than "who may log in."
.TP
.BI valid_duration: " duration"
Should be seconds, not hours: a PAM certificate is validated once,
in-process, and immediately discarded \(em it never enters an agent and is
never reused. Pick this together with the PAM module's
.B skew-tolerance
argument (see
.BR pam_ssoossh (8)).
Default:
.BR 30s .
.TP
.BI extensions: " list"
Should stay empty: interactive-session extensions like
.B permit-pty
are meaningless for a certificate authenticating a single local operation.
Default: empty.
.TP
.BI key_id_template: " string"
Does
.B not
fall back to
.B cert_options.user.key_id_template
the way service's and host's do. A sudo and an interactive login by the
same person need to stay distinguishable in an
.BR sshd (8)
or
.BR sudo (8)
audit log, so an empty value here uses its own hardcoded default instead of
silently inheriting the user template.
.RE
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B ldap
and
.B authentication
keys are top-level (siblings of
.BR http ),
.B not
nested under
.BR http: .
The
.B _defaults.yaml
file compiled into the server currently nests them under
.B http:
by mistake, which means their shipped defaults \(em including
.B authentication.scopes: "profile email"
and
.B authentication.fields.username: "preferred_username"
\(em are silently ignored by the config loader rather than actually
applied. In practice this rarely matters because
.BR authentication.provider_url ,
.BR authentication.client_id ", and"
.B authentication.fields.username
are required and must be set explicitly in every real deployment's own
config file anyway (placed correctly, at the top level, as shown in this
page); but do not rely on the scopes/field-mapping defaults appearing
unless your own config file sets them.
.IP \(bu 2
An
.B authentication.redirect_url
key appears in some older example configuration in this repository. It is
not a recognized field \(em the OAuth redirect URI is always derived
automatically from
.B http.public_url
(or
.B http.server_name
plus the listen port when nothing sits in front of the process). Setting
it has no effect.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ISTERMINAL
Set to
.B 1
to force log output to be treated as an interactive terminal (colorized
text instead of plain/JSON), overriding real TTY detection on stderr.
Useful under a debugger or IDE-integrated terminal that isn't recognized as
a TTY. Not a general configuration mechanism: no other setting in this page
is read from the environment.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssoosshd (8),
.BR ssoossh (1),
.BR ssoossh.yaml (5),
.BR pam_ssoossh (8)
